1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:07,891
Hi everyone! This is GlycoTwin, my project for personalized
post-meal glucose modelling.

2
00:00:07,931 --> 00:00:15,001
Let me show you how it works, what makes it different, and
the research behind it.

3
00:00:16,147 --> 00:00:20,815
The big idea is that a forecast shouldn't be a one-off.

4
00:00:20,855 --> 00:00:26,036
After the same meal, two people can respond very
differently,

5
00:00:26,076 --> 00:00:31,087
so each twin here holds a personal estimate of how strongly

6
00:00:31,127 --> 00:00:35,623
that person's glucose rises per gram of carbohydrate.

7
00:00:35,663 --> 00:00:43,670
Every forecast carries its own uncertainty, and every update
creates a new, auditable version.

8
00:00:44,812 --> 00:00:48,671
This section is the whole story in five steps.

9
00:00:48,711 --> 00:00:52,487
Real data from the CGMacros research dataset.

10
00:00:52,527 --> 00:00:59,862
A simple model: the rise in glucose is a personal
sensitivity, plus an activity effect,

11
00:00:59,902 --> 00:01:02,575
times the grams of carbohydrate.

12
00:01:02,615 --> 00:01:06,221
A forecast of the two-hour glucose maximum.

13
00:01:06,261 --> 00:01:09,951
An observation, once that window has closed.

14
00:01:09,991 --> 00:01:15,038
And a Bayesian update that writes a new version of the twin.

15
00:01:15,078 --> 00:01:20,718
Forecast, observe, update is the loop this project is built
around.

16
00:01:23,593 --> 00:01:26,540
The research summary is honest too.

17
00:01:26,580 --> 00:01:35,841
The first comparison of two model variants was inconclusive,
and I'll show you exactly what that means later.

18
00:01:35,881 --> 00:01:39,169
But first, let me open the application.

19
00:01:40,312 --> 00:01:42,677
I'll create an account first.

20
00:01:42,717 --> 00:01:48,398
Each twin belongs to the person who created it, passwords
are hashed,

21
00:01:48,438 --> 00:01:54,202
and the browser only keeps a session cookie, so I won't
dwell on that.

22
00:01:54,242 --> 00:02:01,499
I'm using a throw-away email on a temporary database, so
nothing here touches real data.

23
00:02:15,579 --> 00:02:18,319
Now I can create a Digital Twin.

24
00:02:18,359 --> 00:02:27,616
For this walkthrough it starts from the built-in
demonstration prior, which the interface labels synthetic,

25
00:02:27,656 --> 00:02:33,177
because participant-level CGMacros data can't be shown
publicly.

26
00:02:33,217 --> 00:02:40,563
The application can also load an aggregate prior exported
from the research pipeline.

27
00:02:41,705 --> 00:02:46,157
This is the twin's state at version zero.

28
00:02:46,197 --> 00:02:57,442
For Model B, the personal carbohydrate sensitivity, beta,
starts at 0.747 with an uncertainty of 0.187.

29
00:02:57,482 --> 00:03:00,729
Model C adds an activity term.

30
00:03:00,769 --> 00:03:05,878
Every update will appear here as a new version.

31
00:03:07,024 --> 00:03:09,703
The other pages follow the loop.

32
00:03:09,743 --> 00:03:13,358
Forecast takes a meal and gives a forecast.

33
00:03:13,398 --> 00:03:18,202
Observations is where an outcome is recorded and applied.

34
00:03:18,242 --> 00:03:26,021
History lists every forecast and every version, and Insights
compares forecast with outcome.

35
00:03:26,061 --> 00:03:27,890
We'll use all of them.

36
00:03:37,800 --> 00:03:42,537
Before the demo, a quick look at how it fits together.

37
00:03:42,577 --> 00:03:51,647
The browser talks to a FastAPI back end, which stores
accounts, twins and every twin version in SQLite.

38
00:03:51,687 --> 00:03:56,424
Behind it is the research engine, with Models B and C,

39
00:03:56,464 --> 00:04:04,384
and the live twin uses the same update and forecast
functions as the research experiments.

40
00:04:05,530 --> 00:04:14,865
The CGMacros pipeline that processes the real data runs
offline, on the researcher's own machine.

41
00:04:14,905 --> 00:04:21,148
Only aggregate priors and reports ever cross over to the
website.

42
00:04:23,495 --> 00:04:26,406
So what's the research behind it?

43
00:04:26,446 --> 00:04:33,023
We use the CGMacros dataset: forty-five participants, about
ten days each,

44
00:04:33,063 --> 00:04:40,802
with minute-level glucose from two sensors, plus activity,
heart rate and logged meals.

45
00:04:40,842 --> 00:04:46,346
It's released under a Creative Commons non-commercial
licence.

46
00:04:47,490 --> 00:04:51,139
Each meal is anchored at its logged meal row,

47
00:04:51,179 --> 00:04:59,255
and the target is whether the maximum glucose in the next
two hours reaches one hundred and eighty.

48
00:04:59,295 --> 00:05:02,370
That's exactly what the app forecasts.

49
00:05:02,410 --> 00:05:09,339
One caveat: the dataset has no meal-end time, so we always
say after the logged meal,

50
00:05:09,379 --> 00:05:11,962
never after the end of the meal.

51
00:05:13,110 --> 00:05:20,487
A meal is only used if its window has enough readings,
missing glucose is never filled in,

52
00:05:20,527 --> 00:05:23,619
and the main set keeps isolated meals.

53
00:05:23,659 --> 00:05:30,624
On the Libre sensor that left one thousand, two hundred and
sixty-two eligible meals,

54
00:05:30,664 --> 00:05:33,838
three hundred and one of them positive.

55
00:05:33,878 --> 00:05:37,465
The Dexcom sensor is a sensitivity analysis,

56
00:05:37,505 --> 00:05:43,811
because the two disagree on the label about twenty-three
percent of the time.

57
00:05:44,956 --> 00:05:53,834
To avoid leakage, features only use information from before
the meal, the folds are split by participant,

58
00:05:53,874 --> 00:06:01,223
and evaluation is sequential, so a forecast only sees
outcomes that were already known.

59
00:06:01,263 --> 00:06:07,508
The leakage check passed for all forty-five participants, on
both sensors.

60
00:06:09,856 --> 00:06:12,311
Now the main demonstration.

61
00:06:12,351 --> 00:06:22,385
I'll enter one meal by hand: sixty grams of carbohydrate, an
activity of 0.3 on the twin's zero-to-one scale,

62
00:06:22,425 --> 00:06:31,256
and a pre-meal glucose of one hundred and ten, for the first
of January at eight in the morning.

63
00:06:32,397 --> 00:06:40,702
And here's the forecast, made from version zero, before the
twin has learned anything about this person.

64
00:06:40,742 --> 00:06:46,801
Model B expects the two-hour maximum to be about one hundred
and fifty-five,

65
00:06:46,841 --> 00:06:54,344
with a ninety percent interval from one hundred and
twenty-six to one hundred and eighty-four,

66
00:06:54,384 --> 00:07:00,282
and a seven point seven percent chance of reaching one
hundred and eighty.

67
00:07:01,430 --> 00:07:07,381
Two hours later the window has closed, so I can record what
happened.

68
00:07:07,421 --> 00:07:11,895
I'll enter a maximum of one hundred and eighty-five.

69
00:07:11,935 --> 00:07:18,233
That's a demonstration value I typed in, not a participant's
observation.

70
00:07:18,273 --> 00:07:22,487
The app only accepts it after the full two hours.

71
00:07:23,637 --> 00:07:27,102
Recording it doesn't change the twin yet.

72
00:07:27,142 --> 00:07:35,309
It waits until I apply it, and a meal with an unusable
window would be excluded instead of used.

73
00:07:36,452 --> 00:07:40,401
Now I apply it. That creates version one.

74
00:07:42,992 --> 00:07:54,263
On My Twin, beta has moved from 0.747 to 0.950, and its
uncertainty has shrunk from 0.187 to 0.144.

75
00:07:54,303 --> 00:08:01,575
The twin is more certain, which isn't the same as more
accurate.

76
00:08:01,615 --> 00:08:08,658
Both versions are in the history, and nothing was
overwritten.

77
00:08:09,802 --> 00:08:18,549
Now the interesting part. Same meal, same activity, same
glucose, but a forecast at noon, made from version one.

78
00:08:18,589 --> 00:08:22,942
The expected maximum is now one hundred and sixty-seven,

79
00:08:22,982 --> 00:08:28,904
the interval runs from one hundred and forty to one hundred
and ninety-four,

80
00:08:28,944 --> 00:08:37,062
and the chance of reaching one hundred and eighty has gone
from seven point seven to twenty-one percent.

81
00:08:37,102 --> 00:08:44,280
The inputs are the same, but the twin's state has changed,
so its next forecast changes too.

82
00:08:46,629 --> 00:08:50,273
Let's look at what the forecast really says.

83
00:08:50,313 --> 00:08:57,977
The target is the maximum glucose in the two hours after the
meal, not a full glucose curve,

84
00:08:58,017 --> 00:09:03,421
so I'm not drawing one. The band is an interval for that
maximum.

85
00:09:04,568 --> 00:09:09,032
The curve on the right is the predictive distribution,

86
00:09:09,072 --> 00:09:14,538
and the shaded part above one eighty is the threshold
probability.

87
00:09:14,578 --> 00:09:20,793
The table gives each model's mean rise, spread and ninety
percent interval.

88
00:09:20,833 --> 00:09:28,801
That interval isn't a guarantee, and a note warns that the
twin has little personal history yet.

89
00:09:28,841 --> 00:09:33,222
GlycoTwin shows its uncertainty instead of hiding it.

90
00:09:35,243 --> 00:09:38,075
So how does Model B personalize?

91
00:09:38,115 --> 00:09:46,783
The rise in glucose is a personal sensitivity, beta, times
the grams of carbohydrate, plus noise.

92
00:09:46,823 --> 00:09:51,631
Beta starts from a prior fitted on other participants,

93
00:09:51,671 --> 00:09:58,005
and after each eligible observation a closed-form Bayesian
update moves

94
00:09:58,045 --> 00:10:04,109
its mean toward what that meal suggests and narrows its
uncertainty.

95
00:10:05,253 --> 00:10:09,660
The forecast then comes straight from that current beta.

96
00:10:09,700 --> 00:10:17,681
Only eligible outcomes can update the twin: the window must
be closed, unusable windows are excluded,

97
00:10:17,721 --> 00:10:20,620
and back-dated forecasts are refused.

98
00:10:20,660 --> 00:10:26,735
So it's a parameter update, not retraining a neural network
after every meal.

99
00:10:26,775 --> 00:10:35,550
People differ, so a personal estimate that learns from each
person's own meals is a natural research direction,

100
00:10:35,590 --> 00:10:40,157
but we haven't shown yet that it beats a population model.

101
00:10:42,506 --> 00:10:45,959
Every forecast comes with an explanation.

102
00:10:45,999 --> 00:10:52,943
Here the carbohydrate term contributes about sixty-three
milligrams per decilitre,

103
00:10:52,983 --> 00:11:02,142
the activity term about minus four, and thirty percent of
the uncertainty is about this person's parameters,

104
00:11:02,182 --> 00:11:05,634
which shrinks as observations accumulate.

105
00:11:06,790 --> 00:11:11,731
This toggle shows Model B and Model C for the same meal.

106
00:11:11,771 --> 00:11:16,535
The research comparison between them was inconclusive,

107
00:11:16,575 --> 00:11:21,961
so the app shows both side by side and doesn't pick a
winner.

108
00:11:23,105 --> 00:11:26,810
History lists every forecast with its status,

109
00:11:26,850 --> 00:11:34,134
and the chart shows beta moving from version zero to version
one while its band narrows,

110
00:11:34,174 --> 00:11:37,380
so you can audit what changed and when.

111
00:11:38,521 --> 00:11:42,062
Insights compares forecast with outcome.

112
00:11:42,102 --> 00:11:47,611
My demonstration outcome of one hundred and eighty-five
landed

113
00:11:47,651 --> 00:11:52,803
just outside the first forecast's ninety percent interval,

114
00:11:52,843 --> 00:11:55,398
and the page says so plainly.

115
00:12:00,280 --> 00:12:04,955
Finally, persistence. I'll log out and sign back in.

116
00:12:09,348 --> 00:12:13,776
The twin is still at version one, with its full history.

117
00:12:13,816 --> 00:12:20,795
That's what makes it a twin rather than a one-off
prediction: it keeps a personal state.

118
00:12:20,835 --> 00:12:23,827
Ownership is checked on every request,

119
00:12:23,867 --> 00:12:31,644
and the project's eight hundred and fifteen Python tests and
thirty-seven frontend tests cover it.

120
00:12:33,990 --> 00:12:43,002
Now back to the research, because the website demo and the
CGMacros evaluation are different kinds of evidence.

121
00:12:43,042 --> 00:12:48,139
The key comparison put Model B against Model C on the same
nine

122
00:12:48,179 --> 00:12:53,032
hundred and sixty-three meals from thirty-four participants,

123
00:12:53,072 --> 00:12:58,333
out of fold and sequential, with participant-clustered
intervals.

124
00:12:58,373 --> 00:13:07,059
These numbers come from our recorded runs in the repository
report; they weren't recomputed for this video.

125
00:13:08,223 --> 00:13:20,286
Model C's area under the curve, which measures how well the
model ranks risky meals above safe ones,

126
00:13:20,326 --> 00:13:24,886
was 0.8059 against 0.7943 for Model B.

127
00:13:24,926 --> 00:13:33,721
The difference, 0.0116, has an interval from minus 0.0084 to
plus 0.0479.

128
00:13:33,761 --> 00:13:39,530
Eight of the nine paired intervals contain zero.

129
00:13:39,570 --> 00:13:51,633
So it's inconclusive: adding activity hasn't been shown to
help, and a small effect isn't ruled out.

130
00:13:52,819 --> 00:14:00,263
Compared with their own never-updated priors, both models
point toward personal updating,

131
00:14:00,303 --> 00:14:06,317
but no interval excludes zero, so that benefit isn't
established either.

132
00:14:06,357 --> 00:14:13,044
The shuffled-history, running-rate and population XGBoost
comparisons are built,

133
00:14:13,084 --> 00:14:20,107
but there's no real-data run in the repository, so I'm not
quoting numbers for them.

134
00:14:22,478 --> 00:14:31,934
To wrap up. What's new here is the integration: a
persistent, versioned twin that forecasts with uncertainty,

135
00:14:31,974 --> 00:14:38,381
learns from eligible outcomes with a Bayesian update, and
explains itself,

136
00:14:38,421 --> 00:14:42,737
all tied to a leakage-safe evaluation on CGMacros.

137
00:14:42,777 --> 00:14:50,230
What the evidence shows is that the Model B versus Model C
comparison is inconclusive,

138
00:14:50,270 --> 00:14:54,324
and that personalization isn't established yet.

139
00:14:55,475 --> 00:15:03,933
The limits: this is an investigational research prototype,
not a medical device and not advice on diet,

140
00:15:03,973 --> 00:15:05,748
medication or insulin.

141
00:15:05,788 --> 00:15:14,329
There are only thirty-four participants, the label is a
lower bound, and there's no clinical validation.

142
00:15:14,369 --> 00:15:19,444
Next, we want to run the full key experiment on the real
data,

143
00:15:19,484 --> 00:15:24,560
and test whether an activity effect differs by activity
level.

144
00:15:24,600 --> 00:15:30,912
Thanks for watching. The code is on GitHub at medhachoudhari
slash GlycoTwin.

